      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-114399",
        "knowledge_point_id": "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/8f20b09e85ce71a1001e7c701ebfafa3e6ac6c8adb29e8dfd47f256f37a29f5f.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,1.5)$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?",
        "worked_solution": "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$",
