      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-89882",
        "knowledge_point_id": "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve",
        "problem": "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)}?$",
        "worked_solution": "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{1}{y})}^{′}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute ${(\\frac{1}{y})}^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{(1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1)} \\\\  & =-1+0+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
