      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-116777",
        "knowledge_point_id": "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864",
        "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step",
        "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3xy,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$",
        "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3xy \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈8.$",
